omr-server (0.1081) unstable; urgency=medium

    * systemd/20-omr-wait-online-any.conf: add --timeout=20, which is what actually saves an ifupdown-managed VPS (https://github.com/Ysurac/openmptcprouter/issues/4367). There networking.service owns the interfaces and systemd-networkd is enabled but manages nothing, so no link ever reaches "routable" and even --any waits out systemd-networkd-wait-online's own built-in default of 120s, on every boot, from inside the boot critical chain (Debian's cloud images order cloud-init-network.service after it): measured on the Hetzner VPS of that issue, "Startup finished in 2.280s (kernel) + 2min 5.439s (userspace)" with "2min 161ms systemd-networkd-wait-online.service", essentially the entire boot. 20s still leaves room for a slow DHCP lease on a genuinely networkd-managed link, where the wait returns in a second or two. The --any -o routable added in 0.1080 covers the other half, one routable link instead of all of them
    * Disable the fail2ban omradmin jail by default (enabled = false). 0.1079 fixed its filter to match only a genuine credential rejection logged by omr-admin itself, but the jail then protects an API whose only clients are the routers of the operator, on a port nothing else talks to, while a false positive still costs a router its VPS: locking the router out of tcp/65500 shows up as "can ping server vps, no server API answer" and is not obviously a ban. The other jails (sshd, openvpn, xray, v2ray, shadowsocks-go) keep their settings. Note the installer rewrites /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/openmptcprouter.conf on every run, so re-enable [omradmin] there after an update if you want it
    * Update omr-admin to 0.18+20260910: fix every boot logging "sysctl: setting key 'net.mptcp.path_manager': Couldn't write 'fullmesh'... ignoring: No such file or directory" while MPTCP silently stayed on whatever path manager the kernel defaulted to. The router sends network.globals.mptcp_path_manager verbatim and that option still carries the out-of-tree v0.95 vocabulary (default/fullmesh/ndiffports/binder), while a mainline kernel registers exactly two path managers, 'kernel' and 'userspace' (net.mptcp.available_path_managers), and rejects any other name with ENOENT. POST /mptcp now maps a v0 name onto 'kernel', all four of them being in-kernel path managers, before the sysctl call and before the value is persisted into 90-shadowsocks.conf
    * Update omr-admin: repair at startup a net.mptcp.path_manager line an earlier version already persisted with a v0 name, sysctl.d being applied at boot long before omr-admin starts, so that line just failed there every time and nothing rewrote it; the corrected value is applied immediately instead of waiting for the router's next /mptcp push. Names the running kernel does advertise, and every name on a kernel that advertises no list at all (a v0 kernel, where net.mptcp.mptcp_path_manager really does take 'fullmesh'), are left untouched
    * Update omr-admin: fix the VPS version reported in GET /config and GET /status (vps.omr_version), which the router stores per server and compares against the latest published version to offer "Update server", so a wrong value means a permanent update prompt or none at all. It was read by scanning /etc/* in glob order and taking the 4th field of any line containing "OpenMPTCProuter VPS": /etc/motd and /etc/motd.head can both carry that line while the installer only updates the first of the two that exists, so a stale version could win on file order alone, and a line where the 4th field is not a version at all ("OpenMPTCProuter VPS is now installed !") was accepted as one. The lookup now follows the installer's own precedence, motd.head then motd, then the rest of /etc sorted, and requires the version token to start with a digit. An empty result is also no longer cached for the lifetime of the process, which is what made a version read before /etc/motd existed (omr-admin is restarted from its postinst, the motd is written at the very end of the install) stick at "" until the next restart

 -- OpenMPTCProuter <contact@openmptcprouter.com>  Thu, 10 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0200

omr-server (0.1080) unstable; urgency=medium

    * Fix the OMR kernel not being set as the default boot entry: GRUB resolves GRUB_DEFAULT against the top level menu only, and grub-mkconfig buries every kernel but the newest under "Advanced options", so the bare entry id matched nothing and GRUB silently booted entry 0. set_grub_default_kernel now writes the full submenu>entry path. On a distribution kernel MPTCP itself keeps working (Debian 13's own 6.12 has CONFIG_MPTCP=y), only the BPF schedulers and syn_retrans_before_tcp_fallback are lost, which is why this went unnoticed.
    * set_grub_default_kernel: the index fallback for menu entries without an id never worked either (${depth:+...} tests for null, not zero, giving "3>00"; the whitespace trim missed grub.cfg's tabs). Both paths are now walked with awk, which handles nested submenus and skips recovery entries. It also looks in /boot/grub2 and /etc/default/grub2, and warns loudly instead of returning silently when grub.cfg or the kernel entry is missing
    * Both install paths now print the running kernel next to the OMR kernel found in /boot and name the release to expect after the reboot: the old advice ("check that a 6.x kernel is used") passed on Debian 13's own 6.12 and could not detect the failure above
    * Install systemd/20-omr-wait-online-any.conf, waiting for one routable link instead of all of them, so a VPS whose IPv6 never becomes routable stops paying the unit's full 90s timeout on every boot. DigitalOcean's own image ships the same override through netplan; "-o routable" is required, wait-online's default operational state is satisfied by a link-local address alone
    * Fix losing DNS the moment the firewall loads when the resolver is reachable only through a second NIC, as on DigitalOcean where systemd-resolved's only upstream (10.114.x.x) sits on the private eth1: the nftables output chain rejected everything not leaving $NET_IFACE, so wget stopped at "Temporary failure in name resolution" while egress on eth0 kept working. The reject is now scoped to $VPNCL_IFACES, the only fw-> destination the Shorewall policy it was translated from actually withheld. Shorewall behaved the same way, so this is inherited, not a regression from the migration
    * Fix systemd-sysctl failing on every boot with net.ipv4.tcp_slow_start_after_idle and net.mptcp.checksum_enabled never applied: shadowsocks.6.1.conf had no final newline, so the MPTCP block omr-admin's POST /settings appends to 90-shadowsocks.conf landed on its last line ("net.ipv4.tcp_slow_start_after_idle=0net.mptcp.checksum_enabled=0", which systemd-sysctl treats as a fatal error). openvpn-tun0.6.1.conf and openvpn-tun0.6.1-ipv6.conf ended the same way, where the client2client sync would have written "bcast-buffers 4096client-to-client" and stopped openvpn@tun0 from starting; all three now end with a newline
    * Fix LOCALFILES=yes, which SOURCES=yes forces on, installing the 6.1 sysctl set on a 6.18 kernel: the local-copy branch had no 6.18 case. Both branches now use the same kernel split
    * Fix a first install on a clean Debian 13 aborting at the omr-admin step whenever that version is not in the apt repository yet: omr-vps-admin Depends on nftables, which the script installed only a thousand lines further down, and the wget+dpkg fallback resolves no dependency, so set -e ended the run with the VPS half installed. nftables now goes in with the early base tools, and that fallback runs apt-get --fix-broken install
    * Fix the VPS being unreachable over SSH from the end of the install until its next reboot: sshd_config was rewritten to Port 65222 without restarting sshd, while the ruleset loaded a few lines below opens 65222 and rejects 22. Only the session running the installer survived, on conntrack, which is why this was never noticed. sshd is now restarted right after the change, and only if sshd -t accepts the configuration
    * Fix a fresh install ending with xray and mlvpn failed although the configuration written for them was valid: each deb starts its daemon on the configuration it ships first (xray on the upstream config.json, geoip:private with no geoip.dat installed; mlvpn before its chmod 0600), which reaches StartLimitBurst, and past that point systemctl restart is a silent no-op. mlvpn0.conf is now chmod 0600 as soon as it is written, and both paths end with a pass that resets the start counter of any enabled and failed service and starts it once more
    * shadowsocks.6.18.conf: drop the five out of tree net.mptcp.mptcp_* keys, which do not exist on any of the upstream MPTCP kernels this file is installed for and only produced a systemd-sysctl warning per boot while setting nothing. The 40 keys left were checked one by one against a running 6.18.41-omr kernel

 -- OpenMPTCProuter <contact@openmptcprouter.com>  Wed, 09 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0200

omr-server (0.1079) unstable; urgency=medium

    * Fix the fail2ban omradmin jail banning routers out of the API they are being set up from: its filter counted uvicorn access-log lines "POST /token" 400 and "GET /login_basic" 401 as failed logins, and both fire without anyone guessing a password -- a router with no VPS key yet (fresh flash, or mid-wizard) polls /token with an empty password and the router's own init retries once per default-route interface, so a single pass could spend the whole maxretry budget, after which the ban rejects tcp/65500 and the router reports only "can ping server vps, no server API answer" (per-WAN NAT addresses made it look intermittent); a browser opening /docs likewise draws the 401 Basic challenge before it can prompt, so six visits banned the operator. The filter now matches one line omr-admin logs itself for a genuine credential rejection ("omr-admin: authentication failure from <ip>", needs omr-admin 0.18+20260908 or newer, see OMR_ADMIN_VERSION), which is the only place the two cases can be told apart -- the access log carries just a path and a status code. HTTP responses are unchanged
    * fail2ban filter omradmin: anchor the failregex ^...$ and set _daemon to the journal's real SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER (omradmin.py, not the unit name) so common.conf's __prefix_line consumes the "omradmin.py[pid]: " prefix; the previous regex was unanchored, which both hid the _daemon mismatch and would have let a crafted request path (access-logged verbatim) forge a match and get any address of the attacker's choosing banned
    * fail2ban: never ban loopback (jail [DEFAULT] ignoreip = 127.0.0.1/8 ::1) -- Debian comments out its own ignoreip and fail2ban's ignoreself covers only interface addresses, while omr-service probes https://127.0.0.1:65500 every 10s and restarts omr-admin when a probe fails
    * Update omr-admin to 0.18+20260908: add the log line the filter above now matches. log_auth_failure() emits "omr-admin: authentication failure from <ip>" at WARNING for a rejected credential pair on POST /token and GET /login_basic, and deliberately stays silent when either half of the pair was empty or no Authorization header was sent at all, which is the distinction the access log cannot express; nothing but the peer address goes into the line, since a username echoed there could carry a newline and forge a second matching line with any address in it. Until now nothing logged a failed login with the client address at all (authenticate_user() only had LOG.debug lines, invisible at log_level='info'), which is why the filter had to read the access log. HTTP responses are unchanged, 400 on /token and 401 on /login_basic
    * Update omr-admin: stop logging a few dozen nft "Could not process rule: No such file or directory" lines per uvicorn process on every startup that happens before /etc/nftables.conf has been loaded -- our own deb restarts omr-admin from its postinst, so the entire startup resync (user_accept, user_dnat, gre_snat, client2client, the DSCP set declarations, dscp_mark) failed against a table inet omr that did not exist yet. Nothing was ever lost, the nftables.service omr-admin-resync drop-in added in 0.1075 restarts omr-admin right after the load and that pass lands the state, so the resync now checks the table is present and otherwise skips the firewall part with one INFO line saying it runs again on that restart; the client-to-client sync of /etc/openvpn/tun0.conf is not nftables state and keeps running either way. A firewall change pushed by the router inside that same window is likewise logged as debug instead of warning
    * Update omr-admin: two more sources of journal noise on a fresh VPS -- nft's stderr is collapsed into a single line (nft prints a diagnostic, the offending input echoed back and a caret line for each problem, and a script can carry one per element, so a single failed apply of the DSCP set declarations alone ran to 30+ lines; identical diagnostics are now deduplicated with an "(xN)" count), and a kernel without MPTCP BPF scheduler support is reported as one INFO line listing the schedulers skipped instead of a multi-line libbpf warning per shipped object per uvicorn worker, that being a property of the running kernel and not of the files. Any other BPF load failure still warns

 -- OpenMPTCProuter <contact@openmptcprouter.com>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0200

